    "authorAssociation": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\r\n\r\n- [X] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\r\n\r\n### Module(s)\r\n\r\nmini.bracketed\r\n\r\n### Question\r\n\r\nHi!\r\n\r\nI'm French and use an AZERTY keyboard. Typing `[` and `]` is not very convenient, so I usually rely on langmap to make `ç` and `à` behave like `[` and `]` in Normal mode:\r\n\r\n    vim.opt.langmap:append({\r\n      \"à]\",\r\n      \"ç[\",\r\n    })\r\n\r\nHowever, this doesn't seem to work with mini.bracketed. For example, `]b` works as expected, but `àb` does not.\r\n\r\nFrom the documentation, I only found that the suffix of mappings can be customized or disabled, but I didn't see a way to change the `[` / `]` prefix itself.\r\n\r\nWhat would be the recommended approach in this situation?\r\n\r\nIs there a way to make **mini.bracketed** work with `langmap`?\r\n\r\nOr is the intended solution to disable the default mappings and recreate them manually?\r\n\r\nWould supporting configurable prefixes (instead of hardcoding `[` and `]`) be considered?\r\n\r\n\r\nTranslated with a LLM.\r\n\r\nThanks!",
    "bodyHTML": "<h3 dir=\"auto\">Contributing guidelines</h3>\n<ul class=\"contains-task-list\">\n<li class=\"task-list-item\"><input type=\"checkbox\" id=\"\" disabled=\"\" class=\"task-list-item-checkbox\" aria-label=\"Completed task\" checked=\"\"> I have read <a href=\"https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md\">CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h3 dir=\"auto\">Module(s)</h3>\n<p dir=\"auto\">mini.bracketed</p>\n<h3 dir=\"auto\">Question</h3>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Hi!</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">I'm French and use an AZERTY keyboard. Typing <code class=\"notranslate\">[</code> and <code class=\"notranslate\">]</code> is not very convenient, so I usually rely on langmap to make <code class=\"notranslate\">ç</code> and <code class=\"notranslate\">à</code> behave like <code class=\"notranslate\">[</code> and <code class=\"notranslate\">]</code> in Normal mode:</p>\n<div class=\"snippet-clipboard-content notranslate position-relative overflow-auto\" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content=\"vim.opt.langmap:append({\n  &quot;à]&quot;,\n  &quot;ç[&quot;,\n})\"><pre class=\"notranslate\"><code class=\"notranslate\">vim.opt.langmap:append({\n  \"à]\",\n  \"ç[\",\n})\n</code></pre></div>\n<p dir=\"auto\">However, this doesn't seem to work with mini.bracketed. For example, <code class=\"notranslate\">]b</code> works as expected, but <code class=\"notranslate\">àb</code> does not.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">From the documentation, I only found that the suffix of mappings can be customized or disabled, but I didn't see a way to change the <code class=\"notranslate\">[</code> / <code class=\"notranslate\">]</code> prefix itself.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">What would be the recommended approach in this situation?</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Is there a way to make <strong>mini.bracketed</strong> work with <code class=\"notranslate\">langmap</code>?</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Or is the intended solution to disable the default mappings and recreate them manually?</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Would supporting configurable prefixes (instead of hardcoding <code class=\"notranslate\">[</code> and <code class=\"notranslate\">]</code>) be considered?</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Translated with a LLM.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Thanks!</p>",
    "bodyText": "Contributing guidelines\n\n I have read CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md\n\nModule(s)\nmini.bracketed\nQuestion\nHi!\nI'm French and use an AZERTY keyboard. Typing [ and ] is not very convenient, so I usually rely on langmap to make ç and à behave like [ and ] in Normal mode:\nvim.opt.langmap:append({\n  \"à]\",\n  \"ç[\",\n})\n\nHowever, this doesn't seem to work with mini.bracketed. For example, ]b works as expected, but àb does not.\nFrom the documentation, I only found that the suffix of mappings can be customized or disabled, but I didn't see a way to change the [ / ] prefix itself.\nWhat would be the recommended approach in this situation?\nIs there a way to make mini.bracketed work with langmap?\nOr is the intended solution to disable the default mappings and recreate them manually?\nWould supporting configurable prefixes (instead of hardcoding [ and ]) be considered?\nTranslated with a LLM.\nThanks!",
